5. Technical Architecture
Frontend Stack Recommendation
Framework: React 18+ with TypeScript
State Management: Zustand (conversations, UI state) + React Query (server state)
Editor: Monaco Editor (via @monaco-editor/react)
Diff: Monaco's built-in diff editor or react-diff-viewer
Markdown: MDX or react-markdown with remark-gfm
Canvas: React Flow (@xyflow/react)
Terminal: xterm.js
Build: Vite or Next.js (if web-first) / Tauri (if desktop-native)
Backend/Agent Layer
Local API: FastAPI or Express server
LLM Clients: OpenAI SDK, Anthropic SDK, Ollama for local models
Context Management: Vector DB (Chroma/LanceDB) for code embeddings
Session Storage: SQLite for conversations, file metadata
File System Integration
Native file watching (chokidar) for real-time sync
Git integration (simple-git) for branch/commit awareness
Project indexing: tree-sitter parsers for code structure
6. Key Interactions & Micro-Interactions
Critical Moments
1. Starting a Generation

User hits Cmd+Enter or clicks "Send"
Input field clears and shows placeholder "Agent is thinking..."
Context indicator updates with files being sent
Streaming begins within 500ms
2. Receiving a Diff

New tab opens in editor with proposed changes
Diff view auto-opens with first change highlighted
Accept/Reject buttons pulse subtly to draw attention
Counter badge updates on file tree showing "3 pending changes"
3. Multi-Agent Handoff

Canvas shows animated data flow along edge
Receiving agent node pulses with "incoming context..."
Previous agent's output appears as collapsed context in chat
New agent acknowledges receipt: "I see [Research Agent] found..."
4. Error Recovery

Error message inline with retry button
"View logs" expands technical details
One-click "Retry with more context" or "Try different model"
7. Success Metrics
Time from spec to first working code < 5 minutes
Agent iterations needed per task < 3
User acceptance rate for generated code > 70%
Multi-agent pipelines completed without human intervention > 50%